@jeffdaily, sorry, have to revert it for now. we hit ~100 build failures in downstream ROCm/HIP code that uses `C10_WARP_SIZE` in host context

the root cause: `C10_WARP_SIZE` now expands to `C10_WARP_SIZE_INTERNAL()`, which is `__device__`-only on ROCm. Any HIPified code calling `C10_WARP_SIZE` from a `__host__` function fails with:
`candidate function not viable: call to __device__ function from __host__ function`

this is a backward-incompatible change: `C10_WARP_SIZE` has been usable in both host and device context since it was introduced. downstream consumers (both internal and external) rely on this, and there's no way to update them all atomically

a potential path forward is to keep `C10_WARP_SIZE` working in host code (e.g. fall back to `C10_WARP_SIZE_UPPER_BOUND` or `at::cuda::warp_size()` in host context) so existing callers don't break, while introducing the new `C10_WARP_SIZE_UPPER_BOUND` / `C10_WARP_SIZE_LOWER_BOUND` macros and `at::cuda::warp_size()` as the recommended alternatives. then deprecate host-side `C10_WARP_SIZE` usage in a separate follow-up once callers have migrated

